{
  "slug": "aesthedes",
  "title": "Aesthedes",
  "year": "1984",
  "subtitle": "The 220 kg Dutch design workstation with 636 dedicated membrane keys, six screens, and a philosophy that designers should never need to learn computers.",
  "why_scout_cares": "This is arguably the most radical keyboard ever shipped. Where the Macintosh promised WYSIWYG through overlapping windows and a mouse, the Aesthedes answered with 636 physical keys — one per function — spread across the entire desk surface like an instrument panel on a ship. Every design operation had a dedicated key: 64 layer-select keys, a colour wheel, boolean operations, move/mirror/rotate. No menus, no modifiers, no overlapping windows. Just your hands moving across islands of membrane switches. The philosophy was as extreme as the hardware: Claessens believed that switching between 'creative brain' and 'cognitive brain' destroyed design flow, so the machine should collapse the distance between intention and execution to zero. The six screens (three 20\" colour, three 12\" monochrome) gave every view its own physical monitor — the UI equivalent of a sprawling physical workbench. The machine was so complex that design agencies hired dedicated operators, meaning designers had to complete their entire conceptual process before approaching the computer. The irony is exquisite: the ultimate anti-menu machine required a human intermediary between designer and screen. Two working units survive at the HomeComputerMuseum in Helmond, rescued from bulky-waste disposal by enthusiasts. It designed the Dutch 25-guilder note, Heineken labels, Volvo dashboards, and — in a fitting loop — its own desk enclosure.",
